{"motion":"This House would allow citizens to vote directly on impeachment cases through a national referendum.","debate_id":"mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::1960","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::1961","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::1962","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::1963","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::1964","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::1965","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::3::1966","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::3::1967","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::1968","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::1969","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::1970","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::1971","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::1972","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::1973","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::1974","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::1975","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::1976","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::1977","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::1978","multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::1979"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::0::1960","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Direct citizen referendums on impeachment reduce the economic burden of prolonged political uncertainty by accelerating resolution of leadership crises. Lengthy legislative impeachment processes often stall policy decisions, deter investment, and disrupt market stability. A national vote provides a clear, timely outcome that restores predictability to governance and economic planning. This efficiency lowers the indirect costs associated with political gridlock."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::0::1961","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"While the Pro side claims referendums reduce economic uncertainty, organizing a national vote on impeachment is itself costly and time-consuming, requiring significant public expenditure on logistics, security, and voter education. These expenses could outweigh the purported savings from faster resolution, especially given the rarity of impeachment cases. Moreover, rushed public decisions may lead to economically destabilizing outcomes if voters lack sufficient information, potentially triggering market volatility rather than restoring stability."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::1962","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Allowing citizens to vote directly on impeachment strengthens social trust in democratic institutions by giving the public a meaningful role in holding leaders accountable. This inclusion fosters a sense of ownership over governance outcomes and reduces perceptions of elite capture or partisan bias in impeachment decisions. When people believe their voices directly shape critical political judgments, it enhances civic engagement and reinforces the legitimacy of the political system."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::1963","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side argues that direct voting on impeachment builds social trust, but in practice, referendums on complex legal and ethical matters like impeachment can deepen social divisions rather than heal them. Public opinion on such issues is often shaped by misinformation, emotional appeals, or partisan media, which may erode reasoned deliberation and mutual understanding. This can polarize communities and weaken the shared norms necessary for stable democratic coexistence, ultimately undermining the social cohesion the Pro side claims to support."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::1964","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political standpoint, direct citizen referendums on impeachment enhance democratic accountability by aligning the removal of leaders with the will of the electorate rather than the interests of partisan legislators. This mechanism reduces the risk of politically motivated impeachments driven by intra-elite rivalries and ensures that the ultimate decision reflects popular sovereignty. It also rebalances power between representative institutions and the public, reinforcing the principle that elected officials serve at the pleasure of the people."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::1965","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side claims that referendums align impeachment with popular sovereignty, but this overlooks the political reality that impeachment is a constitutional check designed to be deliberative and insulated from transient public sentiment. Direct votes risk turning impeachment into a popularity contest, where outcomes hinge on media narratives or short-term political tides rather than due process and legal standards. This undermines the separation of powers by conflating the roles of the judiciary, legislature, and electorate, weakening institutional safeguards against mob rule."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::3::1966","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"From a legal perspective, allowing citizens to vote directly on impeachment through a national referendum aligns with constitutional principles of popular sovereignty, provided it is structured to uphold due process and legal standards. Current impeachment procedures concentrate authority in legislative bodies, which may lack impartiality and transparency; a referendum can introduce a legally binding mechanism that reflects the foundational democratic right of the people to judge their leaders. Properly designed, such a process can coexist with existing legal safeguards by incorporating judicial review or evidentiary thresholds to ensure decisions are grounded in law rather than mere public sentiment."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::3::1967","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that a referendum can be designed to uphold due process ignores the inherent legal incompatibility between direct popular votes and the judicial nature of impeachment. Impeachment requires evaluation of specific legal violations and evidentiary standards, tasks for which a national electorate is neither trained nor institutionally equipped. Unlike courts or legislative bodies bound by rules of procedure and evidence, a referendum offers no mechanism to ensure consistent legal interpretation or protection against arbitrary outcomes. This undermines the rule of law by substituting legal judgment with majoritarian sentiment, regardless of procedural safeguards proposed."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::1968","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Morally, empowering citizens to vote directly on impeachment affirms the principle that ultimate authority over public officials resides with the people they serve. It corrects the imbalance where unelected political elites decide the fate of leaders without direct accountability to the public. This approach respects the moral agency of citizens by trusting them to make consequential judgments about integrity and justice in governance. Denying them this role implies a paternalistic view that ordinary people are unfit to uphold democratic ethics, which contradicts the foundational moral equality of all citizens."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::1969","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s moral argument assumes that direct voting on impeachment respects citizen agency, but it neglects the moral responsibility to ensure that decisions about removing leaders are based on informed, reasoned judgment rather than popular sentiment. Impeachment involves complex ethical and legal considerations that require careful deliberation, not just the expression of will. Entrusting such weighty moral judgments to a potentially uninformed or swayed public risks unjust outcomes and violates the duty to protect individuals from arbitrary condemnation. True moral equality includes the right to fair process, not just the right to vote."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::1970","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Modern digital infrastructure enables secure, scalable, and transparent national referendums on impeachment, overcoming historical logistical barriers. Advances in verified online voting, blockchain-based audit trails, and real-time fact-checking platforms can ensure both accessibility and integrity. These technologies allow citizens to participate directly while maintaining the procedural rigor necessary for high-stakes decisions. Thus, technological progress makes direct impeachment referendums not only feasible but also more inclusive and secure than traditional legislative processes."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::1971","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side\u2019s confidence in digital infrastructure overlooks persistent technological vulnerabilities that could compromise the integrity of a national impeachment referendum. Online voting systems remain susceptible to cyberattacks, voter impersonation, and algorithmic manipulation, with no current technology guaranteeing both universal access and tamper-proof security at scale. Furthermore, real-time fact-checking platforms are often biased or inconsistent, potentially amplifying misinformation rather than mitigating it. These unresolved technical limitations make direct referendums on impeachment unreliable and risky in practice."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::1972","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"The legal framework for impeachment can be adapted to include a national referendum without violating constitutional principles, as long as the process incorporates judicial oversight and evidentiary standards. Such a mechanism would not replace legal due process but rather embed it within a democratically accountable structure. This preserves the rule of law while affirming the people\u2019s sovereign role in holding leaders accountable under clearly defined legal criteria."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::1973","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Con side\u2019s previous turn correctly emphasizes the need for legal rigor in impeachment, but it mischaracterizes a referendum as inherently incompatible with due process. A legally structured referendum could require judicial certification of sufficient evidence before appearing on the ballot, ensuring that only cases meeting defined legal thresholds reach voters. This preserves the rule of law while incorporating democratic accountability, rather than replacing legal judgment with raw majoritarianism."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::1974","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"A national referendum on impeachment can generate economic savings by reducing the duration of leadership crises, which often trigger prolonged market uncertainty and investor hesitation. While the Con side cites costs of organizing referendums, these are one-time expenditures that pale in comparison to the cumulative economic losses from extended political instability. Moreover, a clear and swift public decision provides businesses and financial markets with the predictability needed for long-term planning and investment."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::1975","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side underestimates the economic inefficiency of referendums by assuming swift resolution automatically translates to market stability. In reality, a hastily conducted national vote without thorough public deliberation can produce contested or ambiguous outcomes, leading to legal challenges and renewed uncertainty that further disrupt economic activity. The one-time costs of a referendum also ignore recurring expenses if such votes become frequent, and the administrative burden diverts public funds from more productive economic uses."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::1976","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Morally, excluding citizens from direct participation in impeachment decisions implies a lack of trust in their capacity to uphold justice, which contradicts the democratic ideal of equal moral worth. If the people are deemed competent to elect leaders, they must also be trusted to judge their removal when serious misconduct is credibly alleged. Denying them this role entrenches a moral hierarchy that privileges political elites over ordinary citizens, undermining the ethical foundation of self-governance."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::1977","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s moral claim that excluding citizens from impeachment decisions implies distrust in their moral capacity overlooks the distinction between electing leaders and adjudicating misconduct. Moral agency requires not only the right to act but also the conditions for informed, impartial judgment, which a national referendum cannot reliably provide. Entrusting complex ethical determinations to a potentially misinformed or emotionally driven public fails to uphold the moral duty of fairness to the accused. True moral equality demands robust procedural protections, not just the appearance of participation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::1978","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Direct citizen participation in impeachment referendums can mitigate social alienation by addressing widespread disillusionment with political elites. When people feel excluded from high-stakes accountability decisions, it fuels cynicism and disengagement, particularly among marginalized groups who already experience democratic deficits. Involving the public directly reinforces social inclusion and signals that all citizens, not just lawmakers, have a legitimate role in upholding ethical governance. This can help rebuild fractured trust in democratic systems by aligning institutional processes with the lived expectations of fairness and voice."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_019_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::1979","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The previous Con turn argues that referendums risk unjust outcomes due to uninformed public judgment, but this overlooks how current impeachment processes often exclude marginalized voices, deepening social alienation. Direct participation can counteract this by fostering inclusion and restoring a sense of agency among citizens who feel systematically ignored by political elites. While misinformation is a concern, it is not unique to referendums and already affects electoral and legislative processes; the solution lies in civic education, not exclusion. Denying public involvement on the grounds of potential imperfection perpetuates a social hierarchy that undermines democratic legitimacy."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.88,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.36,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.24,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.05],[0.9,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.25,0.05,0.2,0.0,0.45,0.05,0.12,0.12,0.87,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.18,0.4],[0.0,0.22,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.0,0.02,0.22,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.0],[0.05,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.6,0.05,0.32,0.0,0.35,0.08,0.12,0.42,0.05,0.05,0.34,0.08,0.85,0.82],[0.0,0.2,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.9,0.03,0.88,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.0],[0.28,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.85,0.8,0.2,0.02,0.67,0.0,0.3,0.79],[0.0,0.28,0.0,0.4,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.56,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.64,0.0,0.0],[0.1,0.0,0.2,0.05,0.28,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.3,0.05,0.38,0.0,0.92,0.82,0.08,0.0,0.3,0.04,0.1,0.42],[0.0,0.08,0.0,0.4,0.0,0.88,0.03,0.66,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.58,0.03,0.08,0.0,0.